From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: recover a CTL left in software reset

The blue screen on this machine is one line:

  [dpu error]enc42 intf5 ctl 1 reset failure: -22
  [dpu error]vblank timeout: 208c0
  [dpu error]wait for commit done returned -110
  [dpu error]enc42 frame done timeout

then drm_fb_helper_damage_work spinning on drm_crtc_wait_one_vblank
forever. dpu_hw_ctl_wait_reset_status() found CTL_SW_RESET bit 0 set,
polled 2 ms, and gave up; dpu_encoder_helper_phys_cleanup() then ran and
the pipeline never produced another vblank.

It is not a hibernate bug. It reproduces on a plain cold boot, at the
first kickoff after dpu_kms_hw_init with no earlier DPU error of any
kind, and on the resume path it fires in the same place with the same
ctl. Counted over six boots: two failing boots had it (one cold, one
resumed), four clean boots had none.

Two things can leave the bit set and only one is visible today. Either
the ctl was handed over already in reset -- nothing in this driver has
written CTL_SW_RESET at that point on a cold boot -- or a reset this
driver issued never completed. Log the raw word at dpu_hw_ctl_init(),
where the value can only have come from outside the driver, and stay
silent when it is clear so a healthy boot says nothing.

On the failure path print the raw block words before touching anything:
all-ones across SW_RESET/TOP/FLUSH/START means the ctl is not clocked,
which is a different defect from a genuinely latched reset and must not
be read as one. Then write the bit's own idle value back and re-read. If
it clears, say so and continue rather than tearing the pipeline down.

Bit 0 is self-clearing and MDP drops it ~30 us after the ctl path
finishes resetting, so 2 ms without it clearing means nothing is driving
it to completion; writing 0 restores the resting state rather than
imposing a new one. If the write does not take, the old -EINVAL and the
existing cleanup are unchanged, so a wedged block behaves as before.

Repair here is deliberately at the point of failure and no earlier: it
has to be able to report whether clearing the bit is what brings the
panel back. Hardware verification is required, and a boot that simply
does not reproduce proves nothing -- four of six did not.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_hw_ctl.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_hw_ctl.c
@@ -454,12 +454,30 @@
 		return 0;
 
 	pr_debug("hw ctl reset is set for ctl:%d\n", ctx->idx);
-	if (dpu_hw_ctl_poll_reset_status(ctx, DPU_REG_RESET_TIMEOUT_US)) {
-		pr_err("hw recovery is not complete for ctl:%d\n", ctx->idx);
-		return -EINVAL;
+	if (!dpu_hw_ctl_poll_reset_status(ctx, DPU_REG_RESET_TIMEOUT_US))
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * Bit 0 is self-clearing and MDP drops it ~30us after it finishes
+	 * resetting the ctl path, so 2 ms without it clearing means nothing is
+	 * driving the reset to completion. Print the raw words before touching
+	 * anything: all-ones across the block means it is not clocked at all,
+	 * which is a different bug from a genuinely latched reset.
+	 */
+	pr_err("ctl%d stuck in sw reset: SW_RESET=%#010x TOP=%#010x FLUSH=%#010x START=%#010x\n",
+	       ctx->idx, DPU_REG_READ(c, CTL_SW_RESET), DPU_REG_READ(c, CTL_TOP),
+	       DPU_REG_READ(c, CTL_FLUSH), DPU_REG_READ(c, CTL_START));
+
+	/* Writing the idle value back is the bit's own resting state. */
+	DPU_REG_WRITE(c, CTL_SW_RESET, 0x0);
+	status = DPU_REG_READ(c, CTL_SW_RESET) & 0x01;
+	if (!status) {
+		pr_warn("ctl%d sw reset cleared by hand; continuing\n", ctx->idx);
+		return 0;
 	}
 
-	return 0;
+	pr_err("hw recovery is not complete for ctl:%d\n", ctx->idx);
+	return -EINVAL;
 }
 
 static void dpu_hw_ctl_clear_all_blendstages(struct dpu_hw_ctl *ctx)
@@ -869,5 +887,15 @@
 	c->mixer_count = mixer_count;
 	c->mixer_hw_caps = mixer;
 
+	/*
+	 * Silent on a healthy boot. A value here says the ctl came up already
+	 * in reset -- nothing in this driver has written it yet -- which tells
+	 * a stale handover apart from a reset Linux itself later fails to
+	 * complete. Both have been seen to end in "ctl N reset failure: -22".
+	 */
+	if (DPU_REG_READ(&c->hw, CTL_SW_RESET) & 0x01)
+		drm_info(dev, "ctl%d: CTL_SW_RESET=%#010x at init\n",
+			 c->idx, DPU_REG_READ(&c->hw, CTL_SW_RESET));
+
 	return c;
 }
